Alert Information
Severity: High
Category: Command and Control
MITRE Tactic: TA0011 – Command and Control
MITRE Technique: T1571 – Non-Standard Port
Status: Investigated

Alert Summary

A high-severity alert was triggered due to repeated outbound TCP connections over non-standard port 9999 between internal systems.

The activity was initially flagged as possible command-and-control communication because attackers often use uncommon ports to evade detection.
Initial Observations
Source Host: KUSHAL-PC
Destination Port: 9999
Protocol: TCP
Direction: Outbound
Process Name: tallyscheduler.exe
Process Path: C:\Program Files\TallyPrimeEditLog\tallyscheduler.exe
Telemetry Count: 10
Investigation Steps
Step 1 — Port Analysis

Port 9999 was identified as a non-standard TCP port commonly associated with custom applications or possible command-and-control traffic.

Step 2 — Network Direction Validation

The traffic was observed between internal hosts only. No external/public IP communication was identified.

Step 3 — Process Validation

The activity originated from tallyscheduler.exe. Additional telemetry also showed tally.exe communicating over the same port.

Both executables belonged to the TallyPrime business accounting application.
Step 4 — Traffic Behavior Review

Observed behavior:

low data transfer volume,
internal communication only,
no suspicious beaconing pattern,
no malicious child processes.
Step 5 — Threat Hunting Validation

Additional checks performed:

suspicious PowerShell execution,
malware indicators,
persistence activity,
external callbacks.

No malicious indicators were identified.

Findings

The repeated port 9999 communication was determined to be legitimate internal application traffic generated by TallyPrime-related processes.

Verdict

False Positive / Legitimate Business Application Communication


